14:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1c",null,{"guide":{"date":"May 24–28, 1864","place":"North Anna River to Hanovertown and Haw’s Shop, Virginia","result":"Grant withdrew from Lee’s North Anna position, crossed the Pamunkey at Hanovertown, and continued southeast; the seven-hour cavalry fight at Haw’s Shop gave both armies reliable information about the other’s position","facts":[{"label":"Railroad destroyed near North Anna","value":"At least 8 miles"},{"label":"Hanovertown from Hanover Junction","value":"About 15 miles east"},{"label":"Lee’s withdrawal to Atlee Station","value":"About 12 miles"},{"label":"Distance remaining to Richmond","value":"About 9 miles"},{"label":"U.S. cavalry at Haw’s Shop","value":"About 3,500"},{"label":"Confederate cavalry at Haw’s Shop","value":"About 4,500"},{"label":"Duration of the cavalry fight","value":"About 7 hours"},{"label":"Estimated Haw’s Shop casualties","value":"744","note":"National Park Service estimate: 344 U.S. and 400 Confederate"}],"maps":[{"title":"The battle of Haw’s Shop","caption":"The map shows the roads, woods, streams, initial contacts, dismounted lines, artillery positions, Custer’s final attack, and the Confederate withdrawal on May 28.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/va2119/","image":"/images/haws-shop-battle-map.jpg","alt":"Detailed map of the Battle of Haw’s Shop or Enon Church on May 28, 1864"},{"title":"The movement from North Anna to the Pamunkey","caption":"Grant withdrew north of the North Anna, moved east and southeast, and crossed the Pamunkey near Hanovertown. Lee used shorter interior roads toward Atlee Station and Totopotomoy Creek.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99448882/","image":"/images/overland-campaign-1864-map.jpg","alt":"Historical map of the Overland Campaign movement from North Anna toward Richmond"},{"title":"Hanovertown, Haw’s Shop, and Richmond today","caption":"Haw’s Shop stood between the Pamunkey crossings and the Totopotomoy line. The fight delayed U.S. reconnaissance while both armies moved into positions northeast of Richmond.","source":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright","embed":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/export/embed.html?bbox=-77.62%2C37.53%2C-77.04%2C37.91&layer=mapnik&marker=37.6769%2C-77.3071"}],"moments":[{"time":"1:18","audioTime":78,"title":"Lee held an inverted-V position at North Anna","detail":"The Confederate line met at Ox Ford and extended southwest and southeast. Grant’s two wings could not easily support one another across the river, while Lee could move troops inside the angle."},{"time":"3:52","audioTime":232,"title":"A later account claimed Lee intended to attack","detail":"Charles Venable said Lee wanted Hill to strike Warren’s isolated V Corps but illness prevented close direction. The episode notes that no contemporary order or troop movement clearly supports the claim."},{"time":"6:00","audioTime":360,"title":"The Confederate posture remained defensive","detail":"Hill and Anderson made no preparation for a general assault. Ewell’s limited attack against Gibbon on May 24 avoided a larger engagement."},{"time":"7:07","audioTime":427,"title":"U.S. scouts mapped Lee’s position","detail":"By noon on May 25, probes had established the shape of the Confederate line. Grant told Halleck that Lee was making a determined stand between the North and South Anna rivers."},{"time":"7:53","audioTime":473,"title":"Grant revised operations elsewhere in Virginia","detail":"He planned to withdraw most of Smith’s troops from Bermuda Hundred and send them to the Pamunkey, while David Hunter renewed operations in the Shenandoah Valley."},{"time":"9:46","audioTime":586,"title":"Grant considered moving around either flank","detail":"A western movement could cut the Virginia Central Railroad but would complicate supply. An eastern movement repeated the earlier turns but offered a safer route."},{"time":"10:54","audioTime":654,"title":"The council first selected a western movement","detail":"Grant, Warren, and Henry Hunt initially supported moving around Lee’s left. Grant reconsidered because the army could not maintain a dependable supply line on that route."},{"time":"11:28","audioTime":688,"title":"Grant ordered the army toward Hanovertown","detail":"He gave Meade detailed marching orders for a southeastern movement to the Pamunkey River, about fifteen miles east of Hanover Junction."},{"time":"12:12","audioTime":732,"title":"Wilson feinted against the Confederate left","detail":"Dismounted cavalry and twelve guns created a noisy demonstration on May 26. Lee briefly believed the U.S. Army might move west."},{"time":"12:44","audioTime":764,"title":"U.S. troops destroyed the local railroads","detail":"Work parties burned ties and heated and twisted rails. At least eight miles of the Virginia Central Railroad and part of the Richmond, Fredericksburg and Potomac line were disabled."},{"time":"14:12","audioTime":852,"title":"Grant believed Lee’s army was close to defeat","detail":"Reports from prisoners and Lee’s use of earthworks led Grant to tell Halleck on May 26 that U.S. morale was higher and eventual success appeared assured."},{"time":"16:24","audioTime":984,"title":"The turning movement began","detail":"Supply wagons and artillery moved first. Torbert and Gregg’s cavalry divisions and David Russell’s VI Corps division led the march toward the Pamunkey."},{"time":"17:02","audioTime":1022,"title":"U.S. forces secured Hanovertown","detail":"Cavalry drove away a small Confederate force, engineers built two pontoon bridges, and Sheridan was south of the Pamunkey by 9 a.m. on May 27."},{"time":"17:46","audioTime":1066,"title":"The Army of the Potomac completed the crossing","detail":"Most infantry crossed by noon on May 28. The last U.S. troops reached the south bank shortly after midnight on May 29."},{"time":"18:01","audioTime":1081,"title":"Lee shifted toward Atlee Station","detail":"Once he recognized the southeastern move, Lee withdrew roughly twelve miles. His new position left only about nine miles between the army and Richmond."},{"time":"20:43","audioTime":1243,"title":"Lee sent Hampton to find the U.S. infantry","detail":"Hampton took four cavalry brigades, horse artillery, and three mounted-infantry regiments toward the Hanovertown crossings on May 28."},{"time":"21:19","audioTime":1279,"title":"The reconnaissance forces met near Haw’s Shop","detail":"Hampton’s column encountered Henry Davies’s brigade, the lead of Gregg’s U.S. cavalry reconnaissance, about three-quarters of a mile from the road junction."},{"time":"22:20","audioTime":1340,"title":"Hampton formed a strong dismounted line","detail":"A stream and mill pond protected the flanks. Confederate cavalry and mounted infantry built a barricade across the road, forcing Gregg to attack directly."},{"time":"23:07","audioTime":1387,"title":"Gregg requested reinforcements","detail":"Two U.S. brigades could not break the position. Repeating rifles produced heavy fire, but the dismounted battle remained unresolved for more than five hours."},{"time":"24:24","audioTime":1464,"title":"Custer’s brigade entered the battle","detail":"Meade refused Sheridan’s request for infantry, so Sheridan committed Custer’s available Michigan cavalry brigade to support Gregg."},{"time":"24:39","audioTime":1479,"title":"Hampton began withdrawing after completing his mission","detail":"Captured soldiers confirmed that V and VI Corps were across the Pamunkey. Hampton ordered his brigades to disengage."},{"time":"25:04","audioTime":1504,"title":"Custer struck Butler’s mounted infantry","detail":"Inexperienced Confederates misread U.S. movement as a retreat and advanced. Custer’s fresh brigade counterattacked, captured at least eighty men, and pressed the main Confederate line."},{"time":"25:41","audioTime":1541,"title":"Both sides suffered several hundred casualties","detail":"The episode counts 297 casualties in Gregg and Custer’s commands and says Confederate losses were probably higher. The National Park Service estimates 744 total casualties."},{"time":"26:20","audioTime":1580,"title":"The battle supplied the missing intelligence","detail":"Hampton confirmed the location of V and VI Corps. Sheridan learned the positions of Anderson and Ewell from Confederate prisoners."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"blue","branches":[{"commander":"Ulysses S. Grant","command":"General-in-chief","subordinates":"Selected the southeastern turn and coordinated operations in eastern Virginia and the Shenandoah Valley"},{"commander":"George G. Meade","command":"Army of the Potomac","subordinates":"Directed the four infantry corps and declined to release infantry for the cavalry fight"},{"commander":"Philip H. Sheridan","command":"Cavalry Corps","subordinates":"Screened the march and reinforced Gregg with Custer"},{"commander":"David McM. Gregg","command":"Second Cavalry Division","subordinates":"Henry E. Davies and J. Irvin Gregg brigades; led the main U.S. force at Haw’s Shop"},{"commander":"George A. Custer","command":"First Brigade, First Cavalry Division","subordinates":"Michigan regiments that broke the withdrawing Confederate position"},{"commander":"James H. Wilson","command":"Third Cavalry Division","subordinates":"Conducted the feint west of Lee’s North Anna line"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"red","branches":[{"commander":"Robert E. Lee","command":"Army of Northern Virginia","subordinates":"Withdrew from North Anna toward Atlee Station after Grant moved southeast"},{"commander":"Wade Hampton","command":"Cavalry division and reconnaissance force","subordinates":"Four cavalry brigades, horse artillery, and Butler’s mounted infantry"},{"commander":"Matthew C. Butler","command":"Mounted-infantry brigade","subordinates":"Three South Carolina regiments led in the battle by Benjamin H. Rutledge"},{"commander":"Richard H. Anderson","command":"First Corps","subordinates":"Position identified by U.S. interrogation of captured Confederates"},{"commander":"Richard S. Ewell","command":"Second Corps","subordinates":"Position identified by U.S. interrogation of captured Confederates"}]}],"people":[{"personId":"ulysses-s-grant","role":"Ordered the southeastern movement and believed continued success against Lee was likely"},{"name":"Philip H. Sheridan","role":"Screened the Pamunkey movement and directed the U.S. cavalry reconnaissance","side":"United States","image":"/images/philip-sheridan.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2017896949/"},{"name":"David McM. Gregg","role":"Commanded the division that fought most of the seven-hour battle at Haw’s Shop","side":"United States"},{"name":"George A. Custer","role":"Led the late reinforcement that struck Hampton’s withdrawing force","side":"United States"},{"name":"Robert E. Lee","role":"Held the North Anna position, then moved toward Richmond when Grant crossed the Pamunkey","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/robert-e-lee-1864.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666540/"},{"name":"Wade Hampton","role":"Led the Confederate reconnaissance and obtained the information Lee needed about Grant’s movement","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Evander M. Law","role":"Explained how Lee’s inverted-V position divided Grant’s army at North Anna","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Benjamin H. Rutledge","role":"Led Butler’s mounted infantry during the fighting at Haw’s Shop","side":"Confederacy"}],"losses":[{"name":"John A. Huff","side":"United States","role":"Private, 5th Michigan Cavalry; had mortally wounded J. E. B. Stuart at Yellow Tavern","outcome":"Killed at Haw’s Shop on May 28, 1864"}],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/haws-shop-battle-map.jpg","alt":"Detailed tactical map of the Battle of Haw’s Shop","caption":"The official landscape survey reconstructs the successive dismounted cavalry positions and the final U.S. attack.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/va2119/"},{"image":"/images/overland-campaign-1864-map.jpg","alt":"Historical map of the Overland Campaign route","caption":"Grant left the North Anna and made another wide movement southeast instead of attacking Lee’s prepared position.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99448882/"},{"image":"/images/philip-sheridan.jpg","alt":"Portrait of Philip H. Sheridan","caption":"Sheridan’s cavalry screened the march, secured the Pamunkey crossings, and fought for information at Haw’s Shop.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2017896949/"},{"image":"/images/sheridan-generals.jpg","alt":"Sheridan and U.S. cavalry commanders during the Civil War","caption":"Gregg’s division carried most of the battle before Custer arrived with a fresh brigade.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2009633605/"}],"sources":[{"label":"Civil War Podcast, episode 493","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/2025/06/01/civil-war-podcast-episode-493/"},{"label":"National Park Service: Haw’s Shop battle detail","url":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=va058"},{"label":"National Park Service: Battle of Totopotomoy Creek","url":"https://www.nps.gov/rich/learn/historyculture/tcbattlebull.htm"},{"label":"U.S. Army Center of Military History: The Overland Campaign","url":"https://history.army.mil/portals/143/Images/Publications/catalog/75-12.pdf"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Haw’s Shop landscape survey and battle map","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/va2119/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Overland Campaign map","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99448882/"}]}}]}]
